<h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line</h2>

<p>The doubling of Kobo's US sales is the clearest sign yet that the EPUB-first alternative to Amazon is becoming a real, visible choice for American readers. Combined with the August 3 launch at Best Buy — which literally ended Kindle's sixteen-year retail lock — the momentum is no longer just a niche-interest story.</p>

<p>If you value owning your files, reading open formats, and not being locked to one store, Kobo is now the easiest path to that on a device you can actually pick up in a store. The switch itself is simple: keep a <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free EPUB library</a>, organize it in <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a>, and copy your books onto whatever open device you choose. When the retail turnaround doubles a company's sales, it is worth paying attention — and for EPUB readers, it is worth celebrating.</p>
